From 31578fcc15da9c47fdbf3f7b6e8d043b79b72ac6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thomas Stuart <tjstuart@google.com>
Date: Wed, 17 Jun 2026 23:50:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] Fix privilege escalation in ACTION_CALL intent trampoline

Previously, when a third-party app with the CALL_PHONE permission fired
an ACTION_CALL intent, the intent was intercepted by the Telecom UI
UserCallActivity shim and trampolined to TelecomServiceImpl#placeCall.
Because Telecom UI operates with System UID, placeCall incorrectly evaluated
the request as originating from a privileged system process. This allowed
unprivileged third-party apps to bypass NewOutgoingCallIntentBroadcaster's
security checks, enabling them to silently execute dangerous MMI/USSD codes
(e.g. call forwarding) or auto-dial emergency numbers.

A previous attempt to fix this (which forwarded the calling UID/package
unconditionally) caused a regression (b/524878429) where emergency calls
placed via the Emergency SOS gesture (triggered by SystemUI) would crash.
SystemUI has the START_ANY_ACTIVITY permission which allows it to start
the EmergencyCallActivity alias, but it does not have the CALL_PRIVILEGED
or CALL_PHONE permissions. Overriding the calling identity with SystemUI's
UID caused TelecomServiceImpl to block the emergency call.

This patch fixes the Confused Deputy vulnerability while avoiding the
regression by:
1. Extracting the original caller's UID and Package Name only if the
   target component is the unprivileged UserCallActivity. If the intent
   targeted EmergencyCallActivity or PrivilegedCallActivity, ActivityManager
   already enforced CALL_PRIVILEGED (or trusted bypass via START_ANY_ACTIVITY)
   on the sender, so we preserve the privilege delegation and do not override.
2. Intercepting these extras in TelecomServiceImpl#placeCall if the IPC
   binder caller is identified as Telecom UI.
3. Overriding the binder identity with the actual third-party app's identity
   when evaluating the CALL_PHONE, CALL_PRIVILEGED, and AppOps permissions.
4. Clearing the calling identity when checking AppOps and permissions so that
   Android 11+ package visibility rules do not incorrectly block Telecom UI
   from querying the third-party app's granted permissions.
5. Explicitly passing the unprivileged caller's package downstream to
   UserCallIntentProcessor and NewOutgoingCallIntentBroadcaster, ensuring
   dangerous intents fallback to ACTION_DIAL or are blocked entirely.

Manual Testing:
- Verified the valid use case: placing a standard call using ACTION_CALL
  from a third-party app holding only the CALL_PHONE permission still works.
- Verified Emergency SOS: placing emergency calls via the gesture (SystemUI)
  works as expected and is no longer blocked.
- Verified the exploit cases: placing an emergency call or a dangerous MMI
  code using ACTION_CALL from an unprivileged third-party app is now correctly
  intercepted and safely downgraded/blocked.

